// THE PROBLEM DP SOLVES: naive recursion with overlapping subproblems

// FIBONACCI — naive: O(2^n) time
function fib_naive(n) {
    if (n <= 1) return n;
    return fib_naive(n-1) + fib_naive(n-2);
    // fib(5) calls: fib(4),fib(3),fib(3),fib(2),fib(2),fib(2),fib(1)...
    // fib(3) computed 2x, fib(2) computed 3x, fib(1) computed 5x
    // Total calls: 2^n
}

// IDENTIFYING DP CONDITIONS:
// 1. OPTIMAL SUBSTRUCTURE:
//    fib(n) = fib(n-1) + fib(n-2)
//    The optimal (correct) answer uses correct answers to smaller subproblems.

// 2. OVERLAPPING SUBPROBLEMS:
//    Call tree for fib(5):
//                fib(5)
//            fib(4)    fib(3)    <- fib(3) computed TWICE
//        fib(3)  fib(2)  fib(2) fib(1)  <- fib(2) computed 3x
//      fib(2) fib(1)
//    Without caching: O(2^n)
//    With caching: O(n) — each of fib(0)..fib(n) computed exactly once

// CONTRAST: MERGE SORT — no overlapping subproblems
function mergeSort(arr) {
    // Subproblems: left half [0..mid] and right half [mid+1..n-1]
    // These are DISJOINT — no element appears in both halves
    // No subproblem is ever recomputed => no DP needed
    // Time: O(n log n) regardless (divide-and-conquer)
}

// CONTRAST: GREEDY (Coin Change with specific denominations)
// Greedy: always pick the largest coin that fits
// Works for [1,5,10,25] (US coins) — PROVABLY optimal for these denominations
// Fails for [1,3,4] with target 6: greedy picks 4+1+1=3 coins, DP finds 3+3=2 coins
// When greedy fails -> DP is the correct approach

// THE DP PROMISE:
// Any problem with optimal substructure + overlapping subproblems
// can be solved in polynomial time using DP
// by replacing exponential re-computation with O(1) cache lookups

